ional weight loss", "Low-grade fever", "Longstanding smoking history", "Steroid use"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Central (Brain/Cord) Lesion", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.2803738317757009, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Musculoskeletal features weakly support vascular causes but lack specific correlation to confirm scalenus anticus syndrome."], "evidence_against": ["Sensory loss and pain strongly argue against vascular causes, moderately weakening subclavian steal syndrome."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Compressive Plexopathy / Apical Mass", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "confirmed", "prior": 0.6153846153846154, "posterior": 0.6728971962616822, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Subacute progression weakly argues against cerebral infarction but does not decisively exclude it."], "evidence_against": ["Unchanged symptoms with neck positioning provide weak disconfirmation of apical mass but do not substantially shift probability."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Vascular (Subclavian Steal / Scalenus Anticus Compression)", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.026, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Sensory loss and pain strongly argue against vascular causes, moderately weakening subclavian steal syndrome."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Peripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.021, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Subacute progression weakly argues against cerebral infarction but does not decisively exclude it."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Cerebral Infarction with Subacute Presentation", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.14756517461878996, "posterior": 0.14756517461878996, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Central Nervous System Infection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.059026069847515975, "posterior": 0.059026069847515975, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Central Neoplasm / Metastasis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.03689129365469749, "posterior": 0.03689129365469749, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.9, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.25, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Spinal Cord Compression / Myelopathy", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.03689129365469749, "posterior": 0.03689129365469749, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.7, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.35, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) and unilateral neurologic deficits support a central nervous system infection/metastasis?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms and unilateral deficits provide weak support for CNS lesions but are less specific than expected for apical mass."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the constitutional weight loss and fever combined with unilateral motor-sensory deficit provide the strongest support for compressive apical mass?", "summary": "The combination of constitutional symptoms and focal deficits moderately strengthens the case for apical mass as a compressive lesion."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's bodybuilder anatomy (musculoskeletal pain, weight gain/loss patterns) support scalenus anticus syndrome as a vascular cause?", "summary": "Musculoskeletal features weakly support vascular causes but lack specific correlation to confirm scalenus anticus syndrome."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the focal motor-sensory deficit (2/5 right arm) with concurrent pain and constitutional symptoms argue against a cerebral infarction due to subacute progression?", "summary": "Subacute progression weakly argues against cerebral infarction but does not decisively exclude it."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of sensory loss and burning pain definitively exclude vascular causes like subclavian steal/scalenus anticus syndrome?", "summary": "Sensory loss and pain strongly argue against vascular causes, moderately weakening subclavian steal syndrome."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Compressive Plexopathy / Apical Mass? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "Unchanged symptoms with neck positioning provide weak disconfirmation of apical mass but do not substantially shift probability."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.615], ["B1", 0.256], ["B3", 0.085]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.673], ["B1", 0.28], ["B1.1", 0.148]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers or time-critical conditions identified", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B2) posterior 0.673 < 0.75 AND dangerous alternative B1 (posterior 0.280, danger 0.8) remains unresolved. Tree depth (1) < max (3). No info exhaustion as seen_evidence_ids is empty and estimated_remaining_value=0.55."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.55, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.6728971962616822, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormality of connective tissue'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.615}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Compressive Plexopathy / Apical Mass", "posterior": 0.673, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 6, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Unchanged symptoms with neck positioning provide weak disconfirmation of apical mass but do not substantially shift pro…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B1.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nPeripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy vs Cerebral Infarction with Subacute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal cns myelination, athetosis, autosomal recessive inheritance, confusion or disorientation, difficulty walking\n\nPeripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy vs Central Nervous System Infection:\n  Favours first:  altered sensation in feet, altered sensation in fingers, altered sensation in shin, bell's palsy, bloating\n  Favours second: bladder or bowel problems, including difficulty (or hesitancy) when trying to pee or a feeling of urgency, cognitive impairment, difficulty walking, electrical tingling or shocks down your back, arms or legs when you bend your neck forward, fatigue\n\nPeripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy vs Spinal Cord Compression / Myelopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: band-like chest or abdominal pain, bladder dysfunction, bowel dysfunction, difficulty using arms or legs, electric shock sensations\n\nCerebral Infarction with Subacute Presentation vs Central Nervous System Infection:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cns myelination, athetosis, autosomal recessive inheritance, confusion or disorientation, diffuse spongiform leukoencephalopathy\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nCerebral Infarction with Subacute Presentation vs Spinal Cord Compression / Myelopathy:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cns myelination, ataxia, athetosis, autosomal recessive inheritance, cerebellar atrophy\n  Favours second: band-like chest or abdominal pain, bladder dysfunction, bowel dysfunction, difficulty using arms or legs, electric shock sensations\n\nCentral Nervous System Infection vs Spinal Cord Compression / Myelopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: band-like chest or abdominal pain, bladder dysfunction, bowel dysfunction, difficulty using arms or legs, electric shock sensations\n\nRelated to Peripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy: acquired peripheral neuropathy, autoimmune neuropathy, autonomic neuropathy\n\nRelated to Cerebral Infarction with Subacute Presentation: anterior cerebral artery infarction, brain infarction, middle cerebral artery infarction\n\nRelated to Central Nervous System Infection: borna disease, central nervous system aids arteritis, central nervous system infectious disorder\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Peripheral Neuropathy / Radiculopathy\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Central Nervous System Infection"}
